rationale:
## Verdict
`requirements.ci.review_criteria` is satisfied. Each of its obligations is carried by the prompt template. The template is marked `{impl}` and is delivered by marked code. Tests check the prompt text.

## Implementation
The reviewer's behaviour is set by the prompt. It lives in the `initial_prompt` template in `md/requirements/authoring.md` (section "Prompt template", the `requirements.authoring.initial_prompt` declaration). That declaration has `{impl}` on `requirements.ci.review_criteria`. This is a requirement implementing part of another requirement, so I assessed its text and did not reassess its own implementation or evidence.

The function `initial_prompt` in `src/authoring/mod.rs` (lines 390-411) is also marked `{impl}`. It loads the approved template with `include_str!` and substitutes `{{subject_kind}}`, `{{subject_name}}` and `{{mandatory_inputs}}`. It adds no review rules of its own. The other behaviour it has is covered by its other `{impl}` markers: `requirements.authoring.initial_prompt`, `requirements.authoring.mandatory_inputs`, `requirements.ci.outcomes` and `requirements.examples.evidence`.

The template covers each obligation of the requirement:
- **Assess the subject using context.** "Assess only the named subject, using cited requirements and definitions as context." This also keeps referenced requirements from becoming full subjects. The mandatory inputs supply the subject text, referenced requirements and terminology definitions.
- **Impl completeness and tests.** "check that the code marked {impl} implements every obligation completely and exactly as stated, and that tests cover the interesting points in the design space, including relevant boundaries and failure cases."
- **Marking completeness.** Code that implements part of the requirement without `{impl}`, including code that only has `{cite}`, "is a gap".
- **Unspecified behaviour.** Behaviour in the `{impl}` code that the requirement does not specify is a gap "unless another definition that code implements specifies it".
- **Parts implemented by another requirement.** "assess whether its text carries out that part; do not reassess its implementation, tests, or evidence."
- **No tests.** "assess whether simplicity or impracticality justifies their absence, and explain."
- **Examples.** The template asks for test setup and assertions covering the scenario and expected outcomes, and requires at least one `{test}` reference for a true judgment. It also says "Explain the coverage."
- **Rationale content.** It asks for "source locations" and, for a requirement, to "explain the implementation and testing judgments."

I found no other code implementing the criteria without a marker.
- The skills in `assets/skills/*` and the `md/reference.md` prose only describe or relay the criteria. They belong to `requirements.cli.behavior_review_guidance` and to documentation citations.
- `md/requirements/ci.md`, `cli.md` and `examples.md` cite the requirement correctly. These are requirement-to-requirement relations, not code.

One minor oddity does not affect the verdict. `audits/requirement-rewordings/authoring.md` quotes the old and new template text with live `{cite}` and `{impl}` roles. It is a change log, not behaviour.

## Tests
- `initial_prompt_carries_the_review_criteria` (`src/authoring/mod.rs`, line 808) has a `{test}` marker. It builds the real prompt for a project and asserts that the following phrases appear:
  - the subject line;
  - "Assess only the named subject";
  - "implements every obligation completely and exactly as stated";
  - the boundaries and failure cases wording;
  - the rule for another requirement implementing part of the subject;
  - the cite-only gap rule;
  - the unspecified `{impl}` behaviour rule;
  - the no-tests justification;
  - the example test-coverage wording;
  - "with source locations";
  - the implementation and testing judgments wording;
  - "Explain the coverage."

  This covers each rule that needs explicit wording. It does not assert the sentence "A true judgment needs at least one {test} reference", which `requirements.examples.evidence` also covers. That is a small omission.
- `ollama_cli_delivers_sources_records_fixme_and_keeps_usage_content_free` (`tests/authoring_cli.rs`, line 235) has a `{test}` marker. It checks that the template reaches the model request end to end, including the opening line and the `submit_judgment exactly once` text. It also checks that the substitutions and the mandatory inputs arrive.

The requirement is a prompt-content obligation. Model behaviour cannot be deterministically tested, so checking the delivered prompt text is the practical and appropriate test strategy. I found no FIXME gaps.
